{"findings":[{"finding":"REQ-5 checks staff identities against a 'Meridian Microsoft identity provider', but the accepted ASM-1 says email and password with no social or single sign-on; the spec's own assumption limits ASM-1 to customers, and no answer authorises that.","reqId":"REQ-5","rubric":1,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"The spec says staff 'must' use a Microsoft identity provider, but nothing in the intent, answers, assumptions or the quoted contract text names Microsoft; the contract only says 'configured staff identity-provider authorization URL', so the vendor choice has no anchor.","reqId":"REQ-5","rubric":5,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-1.3 requires 404/405 for any path and method not in the contract, but REQ-2/AC-2.1 requires `/`, `/openapi/v1.json` and `/swagger`, none of which are in the contract, to return 200.","reqId":"REQ-1","rubric":1,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-2.1 checks only that the generated `/openapi/v1.json` 'includes' the contracted operations, so it can drift from the locked contract (for example `/api`-prefixed paths versus contract paths relative to `servers: /api`), even though REQ-1 says 'exactly as written'.","reqId":"REQ-2","rubric":1,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"REQ-9 and the spec's assumptions say cut-off, working-day and holiday values come from deployment configuration with 'no configuration API or screen', which contradicts the accepted ASM-6 that administrators maintain them in the same app on admin-only screens.","reqId":"REQ-9","rubric":1,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"REQ-7, REQ-8 and REQ-13 are marked op MODIFIED, but current behaviour (C-3) shows an empty AppDb and no receipt, expiry or quality behaviour, so there is nothing to modify and the anchors (an empty context, contract text) don't support it.","reqId":"REQ-7","rubric":5,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-7.6 and REQ-7 lean on 'a condition requiring a two-person quality decision' but never define which receipt conditions send a release into the two-person workflow, so it can't be determined which transition from Quarantined applies.","reqId":"REQ-7","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-7.4 and AC-3.2 cover only valid logger and evidence files; there is no AC for a malformed supplier-format file, an unsupported type, an oversize file, or an upload to a receipt that doesn't exist.","reqId":"REQ-7","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-8.1's precondition is a handling unit that is allocated to an order while possibly Quarantined, but REQ-10 allocates only Released stock, so that state can't occur.","reqId":"REQ-8","rubric":1,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"The expiry transition says what happens to Quarantined, On Hold and Released stock but not to stock that is already packed in a sealed box, on a route, Rejected, Destroyed or Delivered when its expiry date passes.","reqId":"REQ-8","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-8.1–8.3 (and AC-12.4, AC-12.5, AC-11.7) are checked through an internal 'email call record' or 'alert row' that no contracted operation exposes, so the outcome isn't observable at a public surface.","reqId":"REQ-8","rubric":3,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-9.1 covers only a repeat with the same idempotency key and same payload; nothing says what happens when the same key is reused with a different payload or by a different organisation.","reqId":"REQ-9","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-9.4's 'response status and order status value reflect the applicable lifecycle rule' names no status code or resulting state for a change or cancellation after Packed, and AC-9.3's 'response record accepts no date earlier than…' names no observable rejection.","reqId":"REQ-9","rubric":3,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-9.1 requires an idempotency key, but no contract anchor shows that the order operation declares an idempotency header or field; adding one would break the rule to implement the contract 'exactly as written'.","reqId":"REQ-9","rubric":5,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"Q-2 requires staff-role enforcement, but the only role-denial AC covers the Auditor (AC-5.3); nothing checks that, for example, a Warehouse Operator calling dispatcher route or override operations, or a non-Quality Officer releasing stock, gets 403.","reqId":"REQ-5","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-11.4 checks only that a driver sees their own route; nothing says what a driver gets when requesting, scanning or syncing another driver's route.","reqId":"REQ-11","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-11.7 covers route changes only before the driver has left for the affected stop; it gives no rejection outcome for changes after departure, and AC-11.6 gives no error for refused or delivered quantities that exceed the packed quantities or for missing signature or photo evidence.","reqId":"REQ-11","rubric":2,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-11.1 puts the 409 failure cases (reused logger, incompatible packing) in 'then' while its given/when describe only a valid box, so the failure condition is never actually set up.","reqId":"REQ-11","rubric":3,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-12.4 puts 'affected stock' On Hold on any excursion trigger without saying what happens to stock that is Quarantined, Expired, Rejected, Destroyed or Delivered, or to excursions found in historical receipt logger uploads.","reqId":"REQ-12","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"Operational thresholds are fixed in the ACs (15-minute silence, 25 percent tier allowance, three consecutive in-band readings, and the 30-day release-expiry window in AC-7.3), while comparable values such as safety margins and attempt limits are configuration.","reqId":"REQ-12","rubric":8,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"'5,000 devices reporting at once' has no reading rate, latency, error-rate or duration target, so it can't be verified.","reqId":"NFR-2","rubric":3,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"REQ-13 says the two-person approval applies, yet AC-13.2 lets any 'authorized caller' supersede a disposition with a single signed decision, which bypasses that approval.","reqId":"REQ-13","rubric":1,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"REQ-13 makes the latest decision authoritative for extant stock, but AC-13.2 returns 409 whenever a decision 'would change' destroyed or delivered quantity, so a partly destroyed or delivered unit has no defined transition; a supersede back to Released also has no defined back-order effect.","reqId":"REQ-13","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-13.3 gives no error for destroying more than the rejected quantity, destroying non-Rejected stock, or omitting the witness, and AC-13.1 gives no outcome for approval by a non-Quality Manager or a failed identity confirmation.","reqId":"REQ-13","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-14.1 blocks only 'held' recalled stock; it doesn't say what happens to affected stock that is already allocated, packed or out on a route when the recall starts, or which status recalled stock moves to.","reqId":"REQ-14","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-14.2 gives no error when a customer's recall response quantities exceed the delivered quantity, and AC-14.1 gives no denial path for a non-eligible starter.","reqId":"REQ-14","rubric":2,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-15.2 and AC-15.3 cover only cross-organisation access; nothing checks that a non-administrator customer user is denied address and team administration, or covers removing oneself or the last Customer Administrator.","reqId":"REQ-15","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-16.2 lets administrators change an organisation's minimum shelf life and deactivate accounts or products (AC-6.1) without saying what happens to existing allocations, open orders, or confirmed routes assigned to a deactivated driver.","reqId":"REQ-16","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-16.2 has no invalid-input or conflict outcomes, such as a negative shelf life, a duplicate account email, or an unknown warehouse assignment, and AC-16.3 says 'permitted date range' but gives no rejection for an unpermitted one.","reqId":"REQ-16","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-17.2 expects 403 for a write to an audit event, but if the contract declares no audit write operation, AC-1.3 requires 404/405 for that path and method.","reqId":"REQ-17","rubric":1,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"'Consequential contracted change or decision' is never listed, so AC-17.1 and AC-17.3 and NFR-3 can't be checked for completeness, and AC-17.2's 'time passes' gives no measurable duration.","reqId":"REQ-17","rubric":3,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-4.1 and AC-4.3 assume email delivery works; nothing covers a failed code or reset-link send, an expired reset token, a reset request for an unknown email (enumeration), or when attempt-limit counters reset.","reqId":"REQ-4","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"Startup relies on EF Core `EnsureCreated` (C-5), which doesn't evolve an existing schema, yet the spec adds the full data model under a 'migration' risk tag without saying how schema changes reach already-created databases.","reqId":"REQ-3","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-3.3 checks for data that is never submitted ('legacy data exists outside the database'), so it passes trivially and verifies nothing.","reqId":"REQ-3","rubric":3,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-6.3 adds profile versions with effective dates but doesn't say whether existing handling units' tier budgets and allowances are recalculated or stay on the version they were received under.","reqId":"REQ-6","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-18.2 requires a contract-defined `server_error` code with no contract anchor, and AC-18.1 doesn't define 'invalid' configuration beyond the existing missing-connection-string check (for example an unreachable database at startup).","reqId":"REQ-18","rubric":5,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"REQ-2/AC-2.5 has EnsureCreated build the schema on a database with no tables, but EnsureCreated writes no EF migrations history, so a later 'reviewed deployment migration' (AC-2.4) on that same database will try to recreate tables that already exist, and the spec never says how the two schema paths are reconciled.","reqId":"REQ-20","rubric":6,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"AC-2.6 starts the new API against an older schema with a migration still pending and only checks that GET / returns 200; it never defines what contract operations return when their tables or columns don't exist yet.","reqId":"REQ-20","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"REQ-9 says to authorize 'only using the roles and scopes stated in the contract' and to add no permission table, but the anchored operations (for example createAccount and listReceipts) name no allowed roles, so the 403 path is undefined for most operations.","reqId":"REQ-27","rubric":2,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"AC-9.1 depends on a 'stock collection' operation with a `warehouseId` filter, but it names no operationId and has no anchor; the only anchored collection parameters are Search, Status, DateFrom and DateTo.","reqId":"REQ-27","rubric":5,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-9.4 depends on 'a contract-defined password-setting operation' with no anchor, and the anchored AccountInput schema has no password field, so it is unclear whether such an operation exists.","reqId":"REQ-27","rubric":5,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-9.2 requires 403 for a customer reading another organization's order, while the contract lists both 403 and 404 for getCustomerOrder and nothing in the contract or the answers picks 403; that conflicts with REQ-1's 'exactly as the contract says'.","reqId":"REQ-27","rubric":1,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"The contract's customer sign-in 'begins one-time-code verification', but the spec never says how codes are issued or delivered, or when they expire or fail, and AC-9.2 just assumes 'a valid one-time code available'.","reqId":"REQ-27","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"REQ-9 denies access to 'disabled' accounts, but no requirement says how an account becomes enabled or disabled (AccountInput has no such field), or what happens to tokens already issued to a customer whose account is then disabled.","reqId":"REQ-27","rubric":6,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-7.1 claims the authorization URL comes back in a response field `message`, and AC-7.3 refers vaguely to 'the response field'; neither quotes the getStaffAuthorizationUrl response schema.","reqId":"REQ-25","rubric":5,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"REQ-4 cites ASM-1 to fix 'a few' at two to five rows, but the accepted-assumptions list is empty, so the spec adds a constraint the human never agreed to.","reqId":"REQ-22","rubric":4,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"REQ-4 seeds only empty tables yet requires references to resolve to existing rows, so when a parent table already holds real data, seeded child rows would point at non-demo records; AC-4.2 also never checks the references in the tables it does seed.","reqId":"REQ-22","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-4.1's 'the rows are based on contract examples' has no observable pass/fail test, such as matching specific example field values.","reqId":"REQ-22","rubric":3,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-3.1 only checks that GET / returns 200 with Seed:Demo true, which tells you nothing about whether SeedData.Run actually ran.","reqId":"REQ-21","rubric":3,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-8.1 and AC-8.2 are near-duplicates, and neither defines what makes an identity-provider setting 'invalid' (malformed URL, unreachable issuer, missing audience), so the failure path can't be tested consistently.","reqId":"REQ-26","rubric":2,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"REQ-10 provisions the initial account but never says its roles or scopes, or where its email comes from, so under REQ-9's email matching it isn't clear that the first account can ever authorize admin operations.","reqId":"REQ-28","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-10.1 is a deployment 'job' check that just reads the account table, with no defined mechanism or artifact in this repo, so it can't be observed at the API surface.","reqId":"REQ-28","rubric":3,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-1.1 asks for every response case of every operation to be tested, including the 500 ServerError responses, which can't be triggered deliberately, so the AC can't be verified as written.","reqId":"REQ-19","rubric":3,"severity":"low"}]}